---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';import {dateStamp} from '../../dossiers/display.js';import {energyModel} from '../../dossiers/energy.model.js';
import {encodeEnergyPayload} from '../../dossiers/energy.payload.js';
const D=bySlug.energy,model=energyModel(),groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))],payload=encodeEnergyPayload(model);
---
<Base title="Energy flows and electricity · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft='<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Environment / <b>Energy</b>' crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/energy/" ogImage="/og/energy.png">
<div class="page energy-page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="energy-report-2026-balance-ktoe, energy-ember-national-electricity-demand-twh, energy-ember-national-carbon-intensity-g_co2e_per_kwh" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Cooking gas, diesel, coal and electricity reach us through different routes. The Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation’s Energy Statistics India accounts follow fuels from production and trade to power plants and final users. Ember’s electricity records add generation, demand and estimated carbon emissions.</p></div><div class="meta"><span>Energy accounts <b>{model.facts.supply.period}, provisional</b></span><span>National electricity <b>{model.facts.demand.period}, calendar year</b></span><span>Rulers <b>Fiscal energy accounts; separate electricity releases</b></span></div>
 <div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Total primary energy supply</div><div class="v acc" id="n-supply">–</div><div class="d">Before transformation; {model.facts.supply.period}</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Final consumption</div><div class="v" id="n-final">–</div><div class="d">End use; {model.facts.final.period}</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Electricity demand</div><div class="v" id="n-demand">–</div><div class="d">Ember national API; {model.facts.demand.period}</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Electricity carbon intensity</div><div class="v" id="n-intensity">–</div><div class="d">Estimated CO₂e per unit generated; {model.facts.intensity.period}</div></div></div></div>
 <section data-inputs="energy-report-2026-balance-ktoe, energy-report-2026-petroleum-ktoe" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>From a kitchen flame to a factory furnace</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>A household sees energy as a cooking-gas refill, a fuel purchase or an electricity bill. The energy accounts follow the physical quantities behind those purchases. Their totals cannot tell us what a family paid, whether the connection was reliable or whether everyone could afford enough energy.</p><p>Fuel can also enter a refinery or a power station before it reaches a user. That conversion changes the product and loses some usable energy as heat. A coal input and the electricity it produces belong to different stages of the account.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>India can import crude oil, refine it and export petroleum products. The fuel-by-fuel balances preserve those flows and signs. Import dependence cannot be established by dividing every gross import by every total without checking the boundary.</p><p>Electricity capacity, generation and demand answer different questions. A growing fleet can produce more electricity; operation, weather, maintenance and fuel availability influence how much it actually produces.</p></div></div></section>
 <section data-inputs="all energy bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Read the ruler before the number</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Energy and electricity have different boundaries.</b> Primary energy enters the system before conversion. Final consumption reaches users after conversion and losses, including the report’s non-energy feedstock use. Electricity is one energy carrier.</p><p><b>Use compatible units.</b> A thousand tonnes of oil equivalent (KToE) compares heat content across fuels. A petajoule (PJ) is another energy unit. Electricity generation uses gigawatt-hours (GWh) or terawatt-hours (TWh); installed capacity uses megawatts (MW). Physical commodity balances also use tonnes and gas volumes, which cannot be added across fuels.</p><p><b>Keep editions and calendars separate.</b> The current report revises earlier fiscal accounts. The API’s earlier edition remains a separate record. Ember’s national and state releases use calendar periods and different coverage; their totals are not forced to match. A source fetch date is not the year the energy was used. These national energy balances do not serve a state balance cut. In the older API, the earlier coal column includes lignite; it stays separate from the later coal-only column. The current report also names industrial categories that were not separately reported in the older API; their earlier cells stay blank.</p><p><b>Totals overlap components.</b> Industry includes its named subsectors. Oil products include their subproducts. Ember’s clean generation includes nuclear; renewables exclude it. Adding a total to its parts would count the same output repeatedly.</p><p><b>A blank is unknown.</b> Source blanks remain empty cells. A published zero remains zero. Where a series ends earlier than its bundle, the state comparison labels its own latest year. Charts do not interpolate gaps.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">An estimated carbon account</span>Ember emissions and carbon intensity follow its estimation method. They are not direct readings from every power station’s stack.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A source-label defect</span>The earlier PJ tables scale electricity-output rows while leaving their GWh heading. Those values are audit-only in the downloads and do not enter charts or physical headline facts. The correctly labelled GWh branch also leaves thermal-fuel output at published zero, so its total is not a measure of all Indian electricity generation.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A separate capacity guide</span><a href="/guides/mnre/">Renewable capacity</a> gives the ministry’s installed renewable stock. <a href="/guides/climate/">Climate</a> covers warming, weather and broader emissions histories.</aside></div></div></section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="energy-report-2026-balance-ktoe, energy-ember-national-electricity-generation-twh" data-prose="data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the latest accounts let us read</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>The primary-supply and final-consumption readings above belong to the same report edition and fiscal year. Their difference includes transformation, the energy industry’s own use, losses and statistical reconciliation. The bridge below shows these entries separately.</p><p>Electricity demand and carbon intensity belong to the national calendar-year release. Read the fuel mix beside the emissions record: lower carbon per unit of generation does not by itself establish lower total emissions.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>The original state electricity release serves capacity, generation and estimated carbon, without state demand. The state plates place power plants geographically. They cannot tell us the generation mix of electricity delivered to each household, because electricity moves between states.</p><p>We do not know household affordability, outages or a resident’s full energy footprint from these balances. The accounts also leave some use unassigned to a named subsector; that uncertainty stays visible.</p></div></div></section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).flatMap(p=>p.sourceBundles).join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.sourceBundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>–</div><div class="c">{p.kind==='history'?'Latest published reading':p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone"><div id={'plot-'+p.id}></div></div><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}; {p.period}. Source totals and components remain separate.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div><div class="now"><span class="mk">As of {p.period}</span><p>The values follow this source release and its own statistical boundary. Earlier editions, overlapping totals and blank source cells remain separately identified in the full register.</p></div></div></figure></div>)}</section>)}
 <section data-inputs="all energy bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings and source limits</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“More capacity means the same increase in electricity.”</b> Capacity is a rated stock. Generation is output over time; different technologies and operating conditions give different output from the same rating.</p><p><b>“Clean and renewable are interchangeable.”</b> Ember’s clean aggregate includes nuclear. Its renewable aggregate does not. Read the source definition before comparing shares.</p><p><b>“Primary supply minus final use is waste.”</b> The account also contains conversion, own use and statistical differences. The report’s named loss entry has a narrower meaning.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“A generating state consumes its own mix.”</b> Plant location and electricity delivery are different boundaries. These tables do not trace each state’s imports from other states.</p><p><b>“Every source balance closes.”</b> Some published historical transformation rows do not reconcile. Workbook bunker signs and missing PJ subtotal cells also need caution. Published values remain intact, with discrepancies listed in the source-quality register.</p><p><b>“The old API and current report are one history.”</b> Revised values change the edition. The downloads retain both; the guide never joins them across the revision.</p></div></div><p>Method: complete observed API cubes are archived before canonicalization. Original Ember API snapshots and state CSV hashes are verified. Official report workbooks are archived byte-for-byte and extracted by named sheets, years, headers and row labels. Replay checks compare every canonical cell with those source records, then test statistical identities separately. The unit conversion uses the workbook’s numerical factor; a contradictory printed conversion note is not used.</p><p>Report publication dates identify editions. The Ember and MoSPI API archives do not provide a publisher revision timestamp; archive capture times are recorded separately. A report foreword date does not establish an API revision date.</p><p>The guide’s scope is energy balances and electricity, including the complete balance chapter and annex. Other chapters of Energy Statistics India contain additional reserve, price, credit, sustainability and international statistics. They are inventoried in the expansion packet and are not claimed as transcribed here.</p><p>Ember estimates greenhouse gases across electricity’s lifecycle, including upstream fuel and manufacturing emissions, and expresses them as CO₂-equivalent. Its state release excludes captive generation included in national estimates. Historical renewable capacity can include upstream imputation, with no flags for individual cells. Earlier reporting combines Ladakh with Jammu and Kashmir for part of the record. These are source-method boundaries; state totals are not forced to equal the separate national release. See the <a href="https://files.ember-energy.org/public-downloads/ember_electricity_data_methodology.pdf">national methodology</a> and <a href="https://files.ember-energy.org/public-downloads/ember_subnational_data_methodology.pdf">subnational methodology</a>.</p><p>Primary source: <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/publications-reports/innerpage/2817">Energy Statistics India 2026, official tables and definitions</a>. Ember: <a href="https://api.ember-energy.org/v1/docs">national API definitions</a> and <a href="https://files.ember-energy.org/public-downloads/india_yearly_full_release_long_format.csv">original annual state release</a>. Report data vintage: <b id="prov-vintage">{model.plates[0].vintageText}</b>.</p></section>
